OL,1,Vk4                               G4/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGITALIAN RELIEF PLANE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pG"WAS SHOT DOWN"
OL,5, A relief plane to Sarajevo which
OL,6, crashed in mountains was shot down, an
OL,7, Italian minister has said.
OL,9,F"The information on the causes are
OL,10,Fvaried," added urban affairs minister
OL,11,FCarmelo Conte. "For the moment, there
OL,12,Fis even the hypothesis of a missile".
OL,14,FEarlier the UN said that the Italian
OL,15,Faircraft which came down near Jasenik,
OL,16,Fin the Bitovnja mountains, had exploded
OL,17,Fbefore hitting the ground.
OL,19,FA UNCR spokesman said there was no
OL,20,Fevidence yet that the plane was hit by
OL,21,Fa missile.
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11805
SC,0005
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                               G5/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGBOSNIA: OWEN WARNS OF
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGTOUGHER UN ACTION
OL,4,D
OL,5, EC envoy Lord Owen has warned that the
OL,6, UN may have to take a tougher line if
OL,7, it emerges that the aid plane which
OL,8, crashed in Bosnia was attacked.
OL,10,FThe Italian plane came down west of
OL,11,FSarajevo yesterday. Its crew of four
OL,12,Fwere killed.
OL,14,FLord Owen told Radio 4 that there must
OL,15,Fbe a limit to the dangers faced by
OL,16,Frelief workers.
OL,17,F
OL,18,F"There sometimes comes a moment when
OL,19,Fthe world looks at a situation and says
OL,20,Fenough is enough. Maybe this might
OL,21,Fprove to be it," he said.
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11806
SC,0006
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                               G6/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGUN LAUNCHES APPEAL TO
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGPAY FOR YUGOSLAV AID
OL,4,D
OL,5, The United Nations will appeal today
OL,6, for more than #200 million to save
OL,7, nearly three million Yugoslav refugees
OL,8, from perishing in the Balkan winter.
OL,10,FThe money will pay for critical food,
OL,11,Fshelter, medical and transport needs
OL,12,Ffor eight months.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FThe appeal will be launched after a
OL,15,Fmeeting attended by Lord Owen and UN
OL,16,Fenvoy Cyrus Vance, chairmen of an
OL,17,Finternational conference on Yugoslavia,
OL,18,Fin Geneva.
OL,19,F
OL,20,FFighting and "ethnic cleansing" have
OL,21,Fdisplaced 2.7 million people.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11807
SC,0007
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                               G7/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGMAJOR DEFENDS CHANCELLOR'S
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGDECISION TO BORROW
OL,4,D
OL,5, The Prime Minister has defended the
OL,6, Chancellor's decision to borrow #7.5
OL,7, billion of foreign currency in an
OL,8, effort to boost sterling.
OL,10,FMr Major, on a visit to north Wales,
OL,11,Fwelcomed the move because it made a
OL,12,Frise in interest rates very unlikely.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FHe dismissed comparisons with former
OL,15,FLabour chancellor Denis Healey's
OL,16,Fborrowing from the International
OL,17,FMonetary Fund.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FMr Major said people wanted inflation
OL,20,Fbrought down and stable exchange rates
OL,21,Fto encourage exports.
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11808
SC,0008
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                               G8/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGCAR SALES CONFIRM
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGECONOMIC GLOOM
OL,4,D
OL,5, August 1992 car sales have fallen short
OL,6, of the 400,000 figure hoped for by the
OL,7, recession-hit motor industry.
OL,9,FBut the 373,804 sales were still 1.6%
OL,10,Fup on the August 1991 total of 367,646.
OL,11,F
OL,12,FOnly a late surge in registrations
OL,13,Fstopped the month from becoming the
OL,14,Fworst August for sales for eight years.
OL,16,FFord UK chairman Ian McAllister has
OL,17,Fpredicted that a recovery is unlikely
OL,18,Fbefore the second half of 1993.
OL,19,F
OL,20,FLabour warned that the industry faced
OL,21,Fmore months of cutbacks and job losses.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11809
SC,0009
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                               G9/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGMITTERRAND WARNS AGAINST
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGFRENCH "NO" VOTE
OL,4,D
OL,5, President Mitterrand has told the
OL,6, French people France will suffer damage
OL,7, of historic proportions if they reject
OL,8, the Maastricht Treaty on EC union.
OL,10,FDuring a live three-hour TV debate, he
OL,11,Fsaid it was important they voted on the
OL,12,Fissue, not on what they thought of him,
OL,13,Fin the French referendum on Maastricht.
OL,15,FIn a live link from Bonn, Chancellor
OL,16,FKohl backed him, denying that Germany
OL,17,Fwould dominate Europe. "No" campaigners
OL,18,Fsaid the treaty was anti-democratic.
OL,20,FBut a BBC correspondent says the debate
OL,21,Fwas inconclusive and an anti-climax.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11810
SC,0010
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4          j7   h4             G10/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~s j7*u?j?h7`s}GEC FINANCE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5p juj5j5j5juGMEETING
OL,4,F
OL,5, Economics and finance ministers of the
OL,6, EC countries are arriving in Bath today
OL,7, for their twice-yearly meeting.
OL,8,F
OL,9,FThe gathering will be hosted by Norman
OL,10,FLamont in his capacity as chief finance
OL,11,Fminister of the country holding the EC
OL,12,Fpresidency.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FTonight's opening dinner will be
OL,15,Ffollowed by serious business tomorrow.
OL,17,FThe main topic of discussion will be
OL,18,Fthe forthcoming French referendum on
OL,19,Fthe Maastricht Treaty.
OL,21,F
OL,22,D]F This Ceefax page is fromG04-09-92
OL,23,D]F Much more at: teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11811
SC,0011
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4          j7   h4             G11/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~s j7*u?j?h7`s}GEC FINANCE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5p juj5j5j5juGMEETING
OL,4,F
OL,5, No official agenda is published for
OL,6, this "informal" meeting.
OL,8,FBut it is understood that the main aim
OL,9,Fis to thrash out contingency plans for
OL,10,Fa possible currency crisis if the
OL,11,FFrench vote "Non" on 20 September.
OL,12,F
OL,13,FMinisters know such a vote would
OL,14,Feffectively kill the Maastricht Treaty
OL,15,Fstone dead and could well throw the
OL,16,Ffinancial markets into turmoil.
OL,17,F
OL,18,FThe fear is that currency dealers will
OL,19,Frespond to the resulting uncertainty by
OL,20,Fselling sterling, and other weak
OL,21,Fcurrencies, and buying German marks.
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11812
SC,0012
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4          j7   h4             G12/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~s j7*u?j?h7`s}GEC FINANCE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5p juj5j5j5juGMEETING
OL,4,F
OL,5, This would push the pound even lower in
OL,6, the Exchange Rate Mechanism and might
OL,7, force the Government to raise interest
OL,8, rates to support sterling.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FIt would also increase the likelihood
OL,11,Fof a realignment of the ERM, with the
OL,12,Fmark being revalued upwards.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FWhatever action is taken, the EC
OL,15,Ffinance ministers will be keen to agree
OL,16,Fin advance on a joint approach.
OL,17,F
OL,18,FBut while the French vote will dominate
OL,19,Fproceedings, the ministers also face
OL,20,Fother thorny problems.
OL,21,F
OL,22,D]F This Ceefax page is fromG04-09-92
OL,23,D]F Much more at: teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11813
SC,0013
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4          j7   h4             G13/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~s j7*u?j?h7`s}GEC FINANCE
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5p juj5j5j5juGMEETING
OL,4,F
OL,5, The most pressing problem is the severe
OL,6, strain on the ERM of the combination of
OL,7, a weak dollar and strong mark.
OL,8,F
OL,9,FSome ministers may express - at least
OL,10,Fin private - their desire to see German
OL,11,Finterest rates come down as soon as
OL,12,Fpossible.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FThe joint statement due on Saturday
OL,15,Fevening is likely to be a bland affair
OL,16,Frevealing little about the discussions.
OL,17,F
OL,18,FMinisters know that any private
OL,19,Fcommitments made over the weekend must
OL,20,Fremain hostage to the French vote in
OL,21,Ftwo weeks' time.
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,119,180,120,250,0,100
PN,11814
SC,0014
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F118A1788527094
OL,1,Vk4                              G14/36
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGFORMER BULGARIAN
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGLEADER JAILED
OL,4,F
OL,5, Bulgaria's former communist leader
OL,6, Todor Zhivkov has been sentenced to
OL,7, seven years imprisonment after being
OL,8, found guilty of embezzling state funds.
OL,9,F
OL,10,FAfter an 18-month trial Zhivkov, 80,
OL,11,Fwas convicted of buying palatial
OL,12,Fapartments and western cars for family
OL,13,Fmembers and favoured aides at a cost of
OL,14,F#12 million to the state.
OL,15,F
OL,16,FZhivkov is the first of the disgraced
OL,17,Feastern European communist leaders to
OL,18,Fbe convicted in a court of law.
OL,20,FHis lawyers say he will appeal against
OL,21,Fthe sentence.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG04-09-92F  teletextarchive.com
